# Switched from our own from-scratch downstream (taoyao-s-oss) kernel to
# the maintained mainline fork (github.com/sc7280-mainline/linux, already
# packaged in pmaports as linux-postmarketos-qcom-sc7280). The
# postmarketOS wiki confirms taoyao boots on this mainline kernel with
# display/touch/WiFi/network/modem working, and it already ships
# arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7325-xiaomi-taoyao.dts, wired into that
# package's build (dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_QCOM) += sm7325-xiaomi-taoyao.dtb in
# its Makefile).
#
# header_version=3 (not 2!), append_dtb=false, and bootimg_custom_args
# with explicit load addresses are all real, confirmed-necessary fixes
# -- see ../../../../README.md's "Session 2" sections for exactly why
# each one was wrong before and how it was diagnosed. As of session 2's
# end, this produces a byte-verified-correct boot.img/vendor_boot.img
# pair via the real, unmodified pmOS pipeline, but the mainline kernel
# still crashes within ~1s of getting control on this specific unit --
# not a boot-chain/packaging problem, needs a serial console (or
# upstream porter input) to diagnose further. See ../../../../README.md
# and ../../../../TODOs/dual-build.md.

# UNVERIFIED: this is an A/B, dynamic-partition (super.img) device, so
# deviceinfo_super_partitions is very likely required for `pmbootstrap
# install`/flashing to work, the same way device-nothing-spacewar (same
# SM7325 chipset) sets it. Do NOT guess a value here — it's a real block
# device path specific to this device's partition table and copying
# another device's value could target the wrong partition. Determine it
# on-device before attempting to flash, e.g.:
#   adb shell ls -la /dev/block/mapper/
#   adb shell cat /proc/partitions
# deviceinfo_super_partitions="/dev/sdXN /dev/sdXN"
